<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Deciding whether a die is biased</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Two dice, A and B, were each rolled \({@nr@}\) times. The tables show the frequency of each score.</p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Score</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">1</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">2</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">3</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">4</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">5</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">6</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency, die A</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fA[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fA[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fA[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fA[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fA[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fA[6]@}</td></tr></table><p></p><table border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><tr><th style="padding:4px 10px;">Score</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">1</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">2</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">3</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">4</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">5</th><th style="padding:4px 10px;">6</th></tr><tr><td style="padding:4px 10px;">Frequency, die B</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fB[1]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fB[2]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fB[3]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fB[4]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fB[5]@}</td><td style="padding:4px 10px;">{@fB[6]@}</td></tr></table><p>(a) If a die is fair, how many times would you expect each score in \({@nr@}\) rolls?</p><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Which die is more likely to be biased?</p><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Which reason supports your answer?</p><div>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) A fair die gives each of the 6 scores with probability \(\dfrac{1}{6}\), so the expected frequency is \({@nr@} \times \dfrac{1}{6} = {@E@}\).</p><p>(b) On die [[if test="is(sw=1)"]]A[[else]]B[[/if]], score \({@bf@}\) came up \({@bias[bf]@}\) times, about twice the expected \({@E@}\). Every frequency of die [[if test="is(sw=1)"]]B[[else]]A[[/if]] is close to \({@E@}\). So die [[if test="is(sw=1)"]]A[[else]]B[[/if]] is more likely to be biased.</p><p>(c) Small differences from \({@E@}\) are normal: no fair die gives exactly the expected number every time. Bias shows as a frequency far from the expected one, as on die [[if test="is(sw=1)"]]A[[else]]B[[/if]]. Both dice were rolled \({@nr@}\) times, so both sets of frequencies add to \({@nr@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
